<h1 class="section">1. Pattern Details</h1>
<h2 class="subsection">About This Pattern</h2>
<p>This sweater is worked seamlessly from the top down in stockinette stitch. You begin at the round neckline with a folded rib collar, then shape the yoke with raglan increases — using short rows along the way to lift the back neck. Once the yoke is the right depth, the sleeve stitches are placed on hold and the body is worked in the round to its full length, finishing with a ribbed band at the hem. Each sleeve is then picked up at the underarm and worked in the round down to a ribbed cuff. The sweater has no seams.</p>
<h2 class="subsection">Size Guide</h2>
<p>This sweater is designed with <strong>[Ease_] cm</strong> of positive ease, to fit a bust of <strong>[bust_cm] cm</strong>. Your finished sweater will measure <strong>[C_cm] cm</strong> around the chest.</p>
<p>It can help to measure a well-fitting garment you already own and confirm that the finished measurements in the table below fall within your preferred range.</p>
<p>The body length, measured from the mid-back below the rib band to the hem, is <strong>[body_length_cm] cm</strong>. This is easy to adjust — simply knit the body a little shorter or longer before beginning the ribbed band at the hem. The same applies to the sleeves.</p>
<h2 class="subsection">A Note on Gauge</h2>
<p>Getting the fit right depends on gauge — and gauge can be surprisingly unpredictable. Even if your swatch matched, the fabric of a finished sweater often behaves differently: gravity, fabric weight, and the sheer volume of the piece can all shift things slightly. Ideally, gauge is measured over a larger area on a garment that has already been knitted, blocked, and worn. That's not always practical, of course — but it's worth keeping in mind.</p>
<p>This pattern uses half-twisted rib (k1 tbl, p1) at the collar, cuffs, and hem — estimated at <strong>[rib_gauge_sts] sts</strong> per 10cm against your <strong>[Gauge_sts] sts</strong> stockinette gauge. It knits up a little tighter than standard 1×1 rib, but gives a neater, more defined edge.</p>
<table class="details-table">
<tr>
<td>Finished chest circumference</td>
<td><strong>[C_cm] cm</strong>  ([bust_cm] cm bust + [Ease_] cm ease)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Length</td>
<td><strong>[body_length_cm] cm</strong>  measured from mid back</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Gauge</td>
<td><strong>[Gauge_sts] sts × [Gauge_rows] rows</strong> = 10 × 10 cm in stockinette stitch</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Needles</td>
<td>One circular needle in your swatch size (used throughout — yoke, sleeves, body, collar, cuffs, and hem rib)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Yarn</td>
<td>300–800 g depending on size</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Construction</td>
<td>Top-down seamless raglan, round neck</td>
</tr>
</table>


